ys of Knowing: Indigenous and Place-Based Ecological Knowledge", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 4229, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "08:40AM", "timeEnd": "09:55AM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ksk28", "firstName": "Karim-Aly", "middleName": "Saleh", "lastName": "Kassam"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Fernow Hall G24", "bldgDescr": "Fernow Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "FRNG24", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "3330", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NTRES", "catalogNbr": "3330", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NTRES", "catalogNbr": "6330", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "Based on indigenous and place-based \"ways of knowing,\" this course (1) presents a theoretical and humanistic framework from which to understand generation of ecological knowledge; (2) examines processes by which to engage indigenous and place-based knowledge of natural resources, the nonhuman environment, and human-environment interactions; and (3) reflects upon the relevance of this knowledge to climatic change, resource extraction, food sovereignty, medicinal plant biodiversity, and issues of sustainability and conservation.\u00a0 The fundamental premise of this course is that human beings are embedded in their ecological systems.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AG, D-AG, KCM-AG, SBA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(CU-CEL, CU-ITL, CU-SBY)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: juniors, seniors, or graduate students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["To appreciate natural resource development from a human ecological perspective;", "To apply the interdisciplinary lens of human ecology to understand human and environmental relations;", "To appreciate the complex interconnectivity between the ecological and the cultural;", "To comprehend that individual actions informed by cultural systems manifest themselves in social structures that rely on ecological foundations;", "To extend the notion of transdisciplinary to include indigenous and place-based knowledge;", "To situate indigenous and local knowledge within a humanistic framework of knowledge generation;", "To illustrate the participatory and experiential basis of indigenous and place-based knowledge;", "To propose a method best suited for researching such knowledge processes; and", "To value the contributions of indigenous and place-based knowledge in the context of socio-cultural and environmental change and natural resource utilization."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 369574, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "4300", "titleShort": "Indigeneity and Decolonization", "titleLong": "Indigenous Peoples and Decolonial Philosophies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 4202, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "04:25PM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "tar37", "firstName": "Troy", "middleName": "A.", "lastName": "Richardson"}], "pattern": "T", "facilityDescr": "Caldwell Hall 400", "bldgDescr": "Caldwell Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "CLD400", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Enrollment is limited to: advanced undergraduates or graduate students. Course requirements differ at 4300 and 6300 levels."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "6300", "type": "W"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "Indigenous Peoples and De-Colonial Philosophies explores the formulations of de-colonization from multiple intellectual trajectories - namely the Fanonian, Latin American and Settler Colonial Studies orientations. The course pays particular attention to some of the central tenants elaborated across these traditions that provide for the philosophies of de-colonizing, placing them in critical conversation with American Indian and Indigenous scholarship. It will examine the differences and commonalities within and across these philosophies for de-colonization, with particular attention to how they describe relations to land and the political, socio-cultural practices for animating de-colonial present(s) and futures.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Demonstrate a fluency in the philosophical formulations that justified/defended colonization - specifically a fluency in the political philosophies of/for dispossession, 2.) the socio-cultural formulations of settler colonialism. Likewise a fluency in the philosophic and theoretical elaborations on de-colonization, specifically 3.) the Indigenous, Fanonian and Latin Americanist positions, clarifying their common principles and diagnoses, differing aspirations and tensions.", "Develop enhanced interpretive abilities through formal presentations and writing assignments.", "Apply pedagogical skills in teaching course content by leading seminar topics.", "Employ sharpened interpretation and critical analysis skills through course writing assignments and structured editorial assistance to 1) concisely convey central argument(s) of texts, 2) make warrantable claims using relevant historical, philosophical, legal and material/empirical evidence, 3) clearly indicate one's positionality in developing arguments."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 354481, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subje